WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.550
A couple years ago, I was able to raise $2
million dollars for my startup, all because

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:05.550
of a single tweet.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.910
There are plenty of entrepreneurs who can
raise money quickly because they already know

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:13.970
all the big VCs, but this was cold outreach
to an investor I’d never met before.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:18.380
But I knew this would work, because I was
using a three step framework that is pretty

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.270
much guaranteed to get you a meeting with
a VC if executed properly.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:27.150
Fundraising is often confusing and frustrating
for early stage entrepreneurs, but it doesn’t

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.150
have to be.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.660
Follow the steps outlined in this video, and
you will be having pitch meetings with plenty

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.440
of venture capitalists in no time.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.280
I’ve raised money 10 different times now,
and even though each time was slightly different,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.440
every successful fundraise follows this same
fundamental pattern.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.379
Today, I’m going to tell you how I raised
$2 million dollars for my company using Twitter.

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:46.930
It’s a pretty funny story actually.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:50.120
So, without further ado, here’s the tweet
that brought in $2 million dollars.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.420
If it looks underwhelming, that’s because
it was designed to be.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:57.789
A venture capitalist had tweeted about some
research they were doing and I simply replied

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:01.700
saying that they missed one important piece
of the puzzle and I could help them.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.639
Even though that tweet is only 17 words, there’s
actually a lot going on there.

00:01:05.639 --> 00:01:08.979
So let’s break it down and work through
what you need to do as an entrepreneur to

00:01:08.979 --> 00:01:11.509
win the attention of venture capitalists.

00:01:11.509 --> 00:01:14.740
Everyone thinks that they need to be a massive
influencer in order to leverage platforms

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:17.689
like Twitter to raise money, but that’s
just not true.

00:01:17.689 --> 00:01:21.899
Most entrepreneurs are far too busy to grow
a serious following on social media and in

00:01:21.899 --> 00:01:25.959
an odd twist, growing a massive following
can actually hurt your credibility.

00:01:25.959 --> 00:01:29.999
There’s a great saying that goes: “The
rhetorical style of any Twitter account that

00:01:29.999 --> 00:01:34.799
continues to gain followers converges on that
of a fortune cookie.” and I’ve certainly

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:36.429
seen this in practice.

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:41.770
Endless growth requires endlessly broadening
your appeal, but that leads to a kind of “lowest

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:45.409
common denominator” type of content that
anyone can understand.

00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:48.200
Entrepreneurs need to be experts in their
field, and if you’re working on something

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.180
truly new and disruptive, there’s a good
chance that the industry is extremely small.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.560
Consider crypto at the early stage: Ten years
ago, most of the conversation about Bitcoin

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.959
was hosted on small forums and completely
inaccessible to general audiences.

00:02:00.959 --> 00:02:05.369
You had to have strong software engineering
chops to even follow basic discussions, but

00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:07.899
that was actually a positive signal to investors.

00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:12.810
So instead of becoming a “fortune-cookie”
account and constantly tweeting: “work hard

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.060
and anything is possible!”

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:19.480
I’ve always focused on talking about complex
topics where I can actually push the conversation

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.000
forward, even though my tweets aren’t likely
to go viral anytime soon.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.510
At the time I posted that Tweet, I barely
had a thousand followers, but you could quickly

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:33.680
skim my profile and know that I had experience
in consumer startups, and that was enough

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.489
to pass a quick check by someone visiting
my profile and wondering “is this person

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:39.489
legit?”

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:43.650
So before you fundraise, it’s important
to really know your industry inside and out.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.500
You don’t need to be creating loads of content,
but if you’re going to use a particular

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:50.519
platform, it’s good to establish a solid
track record.

00:02:50.519 --> 00:02:55.129
And that’s step 1: Become an expert, and
people will want to hear what you have to

00:02:55.129 --> 00:02:56.129
say.

00:02:56.129 --> 00:02:59.669
There is a great saying that goes: “If you
want to be interesting, be interested.”

00:02:59.669 --> 00:03:04.370
- When you’re working on a new idea, you
should be voraciously consuming information

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:05.939
related to that idea.

00:03:05.939 --> 00:03:09.769
Sometimes this means taking classes on the
subject, but other times it’s just taking

00:03:09.769 --> 00:03:14.849
the time to read books, blog posts, and technical
papers to really understand where the cutting

00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:16.760
edge is in your industry.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:21.200
Once you know your industry cold and are ready
to start networking with venture capitalists,

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.959
it’s important to focus on finding “founder
- VC fit”.

00:03:23.959 --> 00:03:28.620
A lot of entrepreneurs get caught up chasing
celebrity investors, but that’s actually

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.110
a huge mistake for two main reasons.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:36.040
The first is obvious: famous investors are
flooded with pitches all day long, so it will

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.380
be hard to break through.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:42.900
But the second reason is actually more important
here: famous investors who don’t know about

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.730
your market, or aren’t interested in it,
are likely to be less helpful.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:51.450
It can be tempting to just look at the Midas
list of top venture capitalists and think

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:55.629
everyone on there would be an incredible addition
to your company, but a VC who’s focused

00:03:55.629 --> 00:04:00.879
on biotech may not be able to help, at all,
with a crypto project and vice versa.

00:04:00.879 --> 00:04:05.019
You need to focus on finding investors who
have specific experience in your industry.

00:04:05.019 --> 00:04:09.652
Look at who’s funding companies that are
like yours and then start to follow and engage

00:04:09.652 --> 00:04:13.650
with these folks, instead of just aiming for
whoever has the biggest name.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:19.130
That’s what I did when I was raising money,
I stumbled upon a Principle at Greycroft Partners

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:22.530
who was hyper focused on direct to consumer
ecommerce companies.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:26.360
His name was Teddy Citrin and he had only
been in venture for a few years.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:31.210
He has a sharp eye for ecommerce companies
and new trends, but he’s not exactly a household

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:33.530
name in Silicon Valley, at least not yet.

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:38.389
That didn’t matter though, because I was
focused on founder-VC fit, not strictly big

00:04:38.389 --> 00:04:39.389
names.

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:44.319
And this is step 2: engage with investors
who are actually experts in your industry.

00:04:44.319 --> 00:04:48.340
When you find investors who are active in
your industry, it can be tempting to try and

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:52.110
pitch them immediately, but coming on too
strong can often be a mistake.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:56.280
I always think of that scene in Star Wars
where Han Solo is flying past Darth Vader’s

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:57.280
ship:

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.340
“Now don’t get jittery, there are a lot
of command ships.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.849
Keep your distance, but don’t look like
you’re trying to keep your distance.

00:05:03.849 --> 00:05:06.550
-- I don’t know, fly casual.”

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:10.530
You don’t want to look desperate for funding,
but you also don’t want to look like you’re

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.990
hiding the fact that you’re desperate for
funding.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:17.550
Just “fly casual” and start having actual
conversations with VCs about what’s going

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:18.550
on in your industry.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:19.550
So that’s what I did.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:21.490
I started engaging with Teddy on Twitter.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:24.129
First by reposting some research he had done
on the industry.

00:05:24.129 --> 00:05:28.979
He had essentially collected every sub-sector
of the direct-to-consumer ecommerce space

00:05:28.979 --> 00:05:31.800
and organized them into a massive Google sheet.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:36.069
His post had done well on Medium, and certainly
didn’t need me to signal boost it.

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:38.800
But this little shout out put me on his radar.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.360
My tweet only got 3 likes, but one of those
was from him.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:47.259
So 5 weeks later, I followed up, by replying
to his original post about the spreadsheet

00:05:47.259 --> 00:05:49.080
saying: “Loved this post.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.319
There’s one category you missed though.

00:05:51.319 --> 00:05:53.220
Happy to chat about it if you’re interested”.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:57.650
Teddy had clearly spent hours pouring over
industry data, and he definitely thought that

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:02.330
he had considered every possible segment,
but I knew he had missed one critical area

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:06.800
and it happened to be the industry I was currently
trying to disrupt: big tobacco.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.669
My co-founders and I had been grinding on
our new idea, called Lucy, for over a year

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:14.729
at this point, and we knew a lot about the
tobacco industry.

00:06:14.729 --> 00:06:19.379
Cigarettes kill millions every year, but the
tobacco industry still generates over one

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:20.930
trillion dollars per year.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:21.949
That’s right, a trillion.

00:06:21.949 --> 00:06:22.949
With a T.

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:28.460
Teddy had outlined dozens of micro industries,
with market sizes as small as $4 billion dollars,

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:30.099
but he had completely missed tobacco.

00:06:30.099 --> 00:06:35.210
There was a good reason for this, no startups
had really attacked big tobacco before, so

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:38.130
there wasn’t much buzz in Silicon Valley
about the opportunity.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:41.199
That was exactly why we liked the idea so
much.

00:06:41.199 --> 00:06:45.860
Smoking was a huge problem and solving it
would clearly have a positive impact while

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.250
still being a good business.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:51.909
Now, I could have just said: “Hey, you missed
tobacco, you should add that to your sheet.”

00:06:51.909 --> 00:06:54.449
but that strategy would have failed.

00:06:54.449 --> 00:06:58.010
Building intrigue is one of the most powerful
communication skills.

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:03.520
I try to do it in every YouTube video I make,
first getting you interested in what I’m

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.110
about to discuss before diving into the details.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:10.460
I knew that anyone who had spent so much time
researching ecommerce companies wouldn’t

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:14.009
be able to resist the opportunity to learn
about a new area they hadn’t considered

00:07:14.009 --> 00:07:15.009
before.

00:07:15.009 --> 00:07:19.590
This is step 3: build intrigue around what
you’re working on so the investor wants

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:20.590
to learn more.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:23.460
There’s a reason why murder mysteries are
so popular.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:27.800
Just open up Netflix and you’ll see dozens
of shows about mysteries.

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:31.169
Humans are hardwired to seek out answers to
unsolved questions.

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:33.380
It’s basic human psychology.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:36.800
But I didn’t just leave him hanging, I offered
a clear solution.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:41.430
A way to make his analysis more complete and
learn about the missing category: just chat

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:42.430
with me.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:44.860
Adding a clear call-to-action there is critical.

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:49.430
Venture Capitalists get flooded with requests
for meetings all day long, and their schedules

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:51.469
are packed with back-to-back calls.

00:07:51.469 --> 00:07:55.800
The easiest way to get a meeting is to offer
something of value in return.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:00.419
Setting time aside for a meeting creates a
cost, so you need to offset that with a benefit.

00:08:00.419 --> 00:08:05.090
Every VC knows that most meetings won’t
result in an investment opportunity, these

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:08.669
meetings are risks that they take in order
to find the next great company.

00:08:08.669 --> 00:08:13.469
As an entrepreneur, you can offset that risk
by guaranteeing that they will learn something

00:08:13.469 --> 00:08:15.629
interesting that they didn’t know before.

00:08:15.629 --> 00:08:20.090
Even if the investor winds up passing on the
deal, they leave knowing more than they did

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:25.090
at the start of the meeting, and that’s
valuable to someone whose job it is to understand

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:26.090
markets.

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:31.490
My plan here worked perfectly, I had established
myself as someone knowledgeable in ecommerce,

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:36.190
found an investor who was uniquely interested
in ecommerce, and then piqued their interest

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:40.200
and offered to help them expand their understanding
of ecommerce.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:45.140
Following these three steps will make cold
outreach to investors way more likely to succeed.

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:48.760
Investors get flooded with pitch decks to
their emails all day long, so you need to

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:54.070
stand out, building relationships slowly and
then leveraging human psychology to ensure

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:58.790
you get a meeting is vastly superior to just
spamming investors and hoping they respond

00:08:58.790 --> 00:08:59.790
to you.

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:03.170
When you start fundraising for your business,
it’s useful to think about the process like

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:08.000
a funnel: at the top of the funnel, you have
all the investors who could possibly fund

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.960
your business, but you need to narrow that
down to just the investors who would be a

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:12.960
good fit.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.540
Once you have that list, you need to get meetings
with them.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:20.140
It’s impossible to get an investment out
of every single meeting, even incredible companies

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:24.840
like Facebook and Google had meetings where
investors passed on the opportunity to invest.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:29.560
So your job is to make sure that when you
reach out to an investor, they take the meeting.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:32.730
Following this 3 step plan will make sure
that you get those meetings, and don’t waste

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.860
your time reaching out to dozens of investors
who just ignore you.

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:40.370
Now, to be fair, when I sent this tweet, I
already had a pretty significant startup experience

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:41.370
under my belt.

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:45.640
I’d co-founded Soylent and raised over $75
million dollars for that business, but I really

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:48.270
think that doesn’t matter all that much.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:52.220
When I was still in college, I used the same
technique to get in touch with Jim Breyer,

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:56.890
who led the Series A in Facebook and I’d
seen giving a talk at Stanford alongside Mark

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:57.890
Zuckerberg.

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:02.700
In 2012, Breyer and his fund, Accel Partners,
were riding high off of the Facebook investment,

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:06.910
but I was still able to chat with him on Twitter,
just by following his work and sending him

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:08.750
the occasional thoughtful message.

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:13.060
It was super inspiring at the time, and a
big reason why I felt that starting a company

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:15.530
in Silicon Valley would be a great move.

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:20.410
So regardless of your background, if you can
become a true expert in an industry, investors

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:21.650
will want to talk to you.

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:22.790
It’s really that simple.

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:26.500
So get out there and start pitching, but don’t
look like you’re pitching.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:27.780
You know, pitch casual.

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:31.490
If you want to talk about fundraising strategy
for your startup, just leave me a comment

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:36.620
below or DM me on Twitter, and please check
out this recommended video, the YouTube algorithm

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:37.820
thinks you’ll really like it.

